      "id": 1,
      "text": "Coxton’s post office was originally called Caxton after the local Cox families and opened on September 16, 1899.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/coxton_kentucky",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Louisville and Nashville Railroad reached Coxton in 1911, spurring timber and farm transport and drawing new residents.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/coxton_kentucky",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Coxton sits atop Pennsylvanian‑age Breathitt Group rocks, where interbedded sandstone, shale, and coal seams formed in ancient coastal swamps.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/coxton_kentucky",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Coxton lies in the valley of the Clover Fork, a tributary of the Cumberland River, shaping early settlement patterns.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Coxton%2C_Kentucky",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Historical coal mining in Coxton has caused soil erosion, stream sedimentation, and acid mine drainage, prompting state reclamation efforts.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/coxton_kentucky",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The area supports Appalachian wildlife such as white‑tailed deer, black bears, and smallmouth bass in its streams.",
